WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.366 --> 00:00:21.058
Many people wonder how it is possible that in 1521, one of the greatest civilizations of the Americas—with millions of citizens across what is now Mexico—was conquered by only a few hundred Spanish troops.

2
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:30.720
The truth is that this army could not have taken control of the territory without the aid of the empire's own subjects, the many people ruled by the Mexica tribe.

3
00:00:30.721 --> 00:00:41.703
Legend says that the god of the sun and war instructed the founding members of this group to build a city on the spot where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus and eating a snake.

4
00:00:41.704 --> 00:00:54.469
This omen was spotted on a little island in the center of Lake Texcoco, and in the year 1325, the people who would become known as "Aztecs" founded the city of Tenochtitlán.

5
00:00:54.470 --> 00:01:04.416
Although many scholars believe this myth was created years later to explain such an odd location, the site of the city did, in fact, bring the Mexica people good fortune.

6
00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:09.635
Over the next centuries, they turned much of the nearby swampland into productive farms.

7
00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:20.568
They also conquered so many of their neighbors that by the time the Spanish arrived, the Aztec empire included land as far north as modern Mexico and across much of Central America.

8
00:01:20.569 --> 00:01:30.785
The Mexica forced the conquered tribes to pay a yearly tribute that included food and luxury goods such as exotic feathers, furs, gemstones, and gold.

9
00:01:30.786 --> 00:01:37.610
One of the main reasons the Mexica went to war so often was to acquire human sacrifices to appease the gods.

10
00:01:37.611 --> 00:01:46.213
They believed the sun would keep moving across the sky and allow life to continue only if fed by the blood of ritual sacrifice.

11
00:01:46.214 --> 00:01:54.101
They also believed the long-absent wind god would someday return to his throne, and that an earthquake would end their world.

12
00:01:54.102 --> 00:01:57.656
But it was not an earthquake that destroyed the Mexica empire.

13
00:01:57.657 --> 00:02:05.533
In 1504, Hernán Cortés had sailed from Spain to the New World at the age of 19 in search of fortune and adventure.

14
00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:11.032
After arriving on Hispaniola, he rose in rank in the government of the island.

15
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:20.247
In October 1518, the island's governor, Diego Velázquez, chose Cortés to head an expedition to colonize "New Spain."

16
00:02:20.248 --> 00:02:30.462
But the governor became concerned that Cortés' leadership skills, cleverness, and speaking ability might make the young man more popular and powerful than the governor himself.

17
00:02:30.463 --> 00:02:34.947
So Velázquez changed his mind, choosing someone else to lead the mission.

18
00:02:34.948 --> 00:02:40.283
Cortés knew of the governor's decision but left Hispaniola before anyone could stop him.

19
00:02:40.284 --> 00:02:48.221
With several hundred troops on 11 ships, Cortés sailed for the Yucatán on February 18, 1519.

20
00:02:48.222 --> 00:02:55.165
Upon his arrival, he founded the city of Veracruz and burned his ships so his troops could not turn back.

21
00:02:55.166 --> 00:03:00.110
He began to explore the territory, moving inland and meeting with the local people.

22
00:03:00.111 --> 00:03:06.152
Cortés quickly realized that many of these groups were unhappy with the rule of the wealthy and powerful Mexica.

23
00:03:06.153 --> 00:03:12.913
He knew he was outnumbered by the Mexica, so he began to make alliances with their enemies and the tribes they had conquered.

24
00:03:12.914 --> 00:03:24.179
The Tlaxcala, for example, saw that the Spaniards had guns, cannons, and horses—which the Mexica did not have—and the tribe became one of their strongest allies.

25
00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:29.575
One thousand of its members joined Cortés when he began his march to Tenochtitlán.

26
00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:36.916
On November 8, 1519, the force arrived in the capital. They were greeted by the emperor, Montezuma II.

27
00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:42.849
It is possible that Montezuma thought Cortés was the wind god coming to reclaim his throne.

28
00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:51.387
The god was supposed to have fair skin and a beard, and come from the east in that very year. Cortés' arrival fit these predictions perfectly.

29
00:03:51.388 --> 00:03:59.998
Montezuma, reluctant to hand over his power, tried to persuade the Spaniards to leave with gifts of gold. But Cortés and his troops stayed.

30
00:03:59.999 --> 00:04:10.264
Cortés was Montezuma's guest for only six days before he took the emperor prisoner and forced him to swear allegiance to the ruler of the Spanish empire, Charles V.

31
00:04:10.265 --> 00:04:15.216
About six months later, Velázquez sent a small army to arrest Cortés.

32
00:04:15.217 --> 00:04:20.575
When Cortés heard the news, he decided not to wait for the force to reach the capital.

33
00:04:20.576 --> 00:04:27.191
He set off to meet them, leaving behind less than a hundred Spanish troops and several hundred native allies in the city.

34
00:04:27.192 --> 00:04:36.191
He attacked the army in the middle of the night, won the battle, and by telling stories of the gold available in Tenochtitlán, convinced many of his pursuers to join him.

35
00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:45.353
On returning to Tenochtitlán, Cortés discovered that while he had been away, his men in the capital had massacred dozens of Mexica people during a festival.

36
00:04:45.354 --> 00:04:49.351
The Mexica retaliated by placing the Spaniards under siege.

37
00:04:49.352 --> 00:04:55.500
They were so enraged that even an appeal for calm made by Montezuma himself did not quiet them.

38
00:04:55.501 --> 00:05:03.609
Some scholars believe that during his speech, one of Montezuma's own people threw a stone at the emperor, causing an injury that eventually killed him.

39
00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:08.949
Others believe the Spaniards saw that he was no longer useful and killed him themselves.

40
00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:17.711
Whatever the cause, Montezuma II died within a few days of Cortés' return to Tenochtitlán, and a new emperor was chosen.

41
00:05:17.712 --> 00:05:25.815
The siege continued, and without food or other supplies, Cortés and his troops had no choice but to flee the island under the cover of night.

42
00:05:25.816 --> 00:05:33.270
During the retreat, many of Cortés' men were killed; even more drowned because the gold in their pockets made them too heavy to swim.

43
00:05:33.271 --> 00:05:37.428
Cortés, however, planned to return and began to rebuild his army.

44
00:05:37.429 --> 00:05:45.403
It would eventually include thousands of native troops, which helps explain how a few hundred Spaniards could conquer such a huge civilization.

45
00:05:45.404 --> 00:05:51.181
Meanwhile, starting in September of 1520, a mysterious illness was ravaging the Mexica.

46
00:05:51.182 --> 00:05:57.183
The Spanish had brought smallpox to the New World, and people there had no resistance to this disease.

47
00:05:57.184 --> 00:06:09.671
By December, Cortés felt ready to return to Tenochtitlán, but rather than attack the island capital directly, he first conquered the surrounding territories, cutting off Tenochtitlán's food and water.

48
00:06:09.672 --> 00:06:16.171
When Cortés reached the capital, the combination of smallpox and low supplies had weakened the city.

49
00:06:16.172 --> 00:06:27.390
After intense fighting, on August 13, 1521, the empire of the Mexica fell, and Cortés immediately began building what would become Mexico City on top of Tenochtitlán.

50
00:06:27.391 --> 00:06:35.310
Despite Cortés' successes in the name of the Spanish crown, he never received much credit for the conquest of the Mexica during his lifetime.

51
00:06:35.311 --> 00:06:43.523
For the rest of his life, Cortés had to battle his enemies at the Spanish court and in the New World. He died disillusioned and in debt.

52
00:06:43.524 --> 00:06:47.729
But the people Cortés conquered have managed to keep their culture alive.

53
00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:52.412
The spirit of the Mexica lives on in the people of the modern country of Mexico.

54
00:06:52.413 --> 00:07:02.621
The discovery of an enormous Aztec calendar stone, which described how the Mexica viewed their world, spurred a new interest in Aztec culture, religion, and ways of life.

55
00:07:02.645 --> 00:07:13.540
The people who still speak the Aztec language today are proof that although the Spanish took control of Tenochtitlán nearly five hundred years ago, they never really conquered the Mexica people.
